          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional and relational state. Her aside also represents the feeling as real but more substantial than she can or will put into competitive rhetoric.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"since, I am sure, my love’s\\nMore ponderous than my tongue.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression is immediately qualified by “According to my bond,” so it affirms Cordelia’s existing filial bond and positions her love as governed by the duties arising from that bond. Her subsequent explanation confirms that she is returning the fitting duties of a daughter. This is not a new promise, but the focal avowal itself invokes and affirms an existing relational obligation rather than merely describing one.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I\\nReturn those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The utterance is a controlled, measured answer that Cordelia explicitly limits by her bond and by “no more nor less.” The evidence does not present it as an involuntary or reflex-like discharge of affect.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love and affirming a filial bond, the occurrence has a corrective and resistant function: Cordelia answers Lear’s demand while refusing to exaggerate, quantify herself above her sisters, or claim that filial love excludes all other legitimate bonds.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Why have my sisters husbands if they say\\nThey love you all?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public division of his kingdom, Lear asks each daughter to say how much she loves him, with greater bounty at stake. After Goneril and Regan give expansive declarations, Cordelia first answers “Nothing” and is ordered to speak again. She then gives the focal avowal as a deliberately bounded statement of filial love.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia affirms an existing filial undertaking: she is bound to love, obey, and honour Lear in the measure fitting a daughter. The expression does not create a new promise or specify future acts. Lear does not accept it as an adequate response to his love test; he urges her to “Mend your speech,” showing negative uptake of its deliberately limited formulation, though his reaction does not erase its bond-affirming force.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied evidence is recognisable as Act I, Scene I of William Shakespeare’s King Lear. The assessment uses only the supplied passages.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly represents Cordelia’s affection through her aside and establishes the filial bond she invokes. It does not provide detailed terms for that bond beyond the duties she names, nor does it show any later resolution or performance of those duties.\"}"
        }
